<!-- doc/src/sgml/extvacuumstatistics.sgml -->

<sect1 id="extvacuumstatistics" xreflabel="ext_vacuum_statistics">
 <title>ext_vacuum_statistics &mdash; extended vacuum statistics</title>

 <indexterm zone="extvacuumstatistics">
  <primary>ext_vacuum_statistics</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  The <filename>ext_vacuum_statistics</filename> module provides
  extended per-table, per-index, and per-database vacuum statistics
  (buffer I/O, WAL, general, timing) via views in the
  <literal>ext_vacuum_statistics</literal> schema.
 </para>

 <para>
  The module must be loaded by adding <literal>ext_vacuum_statistics</literal> to
  <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/> in
  <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>, because it registers a vacuum hook at
  server startup.  This means that a server restart is needed to add or remove
  the module.  After installation, run
  <command>CREATE EXTENSION ext_vacuum_statistics</command> in each database
  where you want to use it.
 </para>

 <para>
  Note that starting the server without the module in
  <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/>, even once, discards
  <emphasis>all</emphasis> cumulative statistics, not only those of this
  module.  The statistics file then holds entries of a statistics kind that
  is not registered, so the server reports the file as corrupted and resets
  every statistics kind, built-in ones such as those shown in
  <structname>pg_stat_user_tables</structname> included.  To stop collecting
  extension resource statistics without that side effect, keep the module loaded and
  set <varname>vacuum_statistics.enabled</varname> to <literal>off</literal>
  instead.
 </para>

 <para>
  When active, the module provides views
  <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_tables</structname>,
  <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_ao_tables</structname>,
  <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_indexes</structname>, and
  <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_database</structname>,
  plus functions to reset statistics and manage tracking.
 </para>

 <para>
  Each tracked object (one table or one index) uses a fixed-size shared
  memory entry; the exact size depends on the platform.
 </para>

 <para>
  The statistics of a table or an index are dropped together with it, when
  the transaction that drops it commits, and a new table or index starts
  from empty statistics even if it gets the OID of an old one; both work
  the way they do for the built-in relation statistics.  The module does
  that with an <literal>object_access_hook</literal>, so the statistics file
  does not accumulate the entries of relations long gone.  The statistics
  of a dropped database are dropped along with the rest of its cumulative
  statistics.
 </para>

 <para>
  Work counters are accumulated only by native pgstat. The module's table and
  index views read the same counters as the native VACUUM views via
  <function>pg_stat_get_vacuum_stats()</function>, and add separately stored
  resource measurements. Work obeys <varname>track_counts</varname>;
  <varname>vacuum_statistics.enabled</varname> controls only extension metrics,
  including AO phase resources and the awaiting-drop snapshot. Work remains
  visible when extension collection is disabled. Relations without a resource
  entry show zero resource counters.
 </para>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-tables">
  <title>The <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_tables</structname> View</title>

  <indexterm zone="extvacuumstatistics">
   <secondary>pg_stats_vacuum_tables</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   The view <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_tables</structname>
   contains one row for each table in the current database (including
   materialized views, TOAST tables and AO auxiliary tables), showing statistics about vacuuming that specific table.  The columns
   are shown in <xref linkend="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-tables-columns"/>.
   AO phase resources are exposed separately by
   <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_ao_tables</structname>;
   they are not columns of this general table view.
  </para>

  <table id="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-tables-columns">
   <title><structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_tables</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="1">
    <thead>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       Column Type
      </para>
      <para>
       Description
      </para></entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>relid</structfield> <type>oid</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       OID of a table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>schema</structfield> <type>name</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Name of the schema this table is in
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>relname</structfield> <type>name</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Name of this table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>dbname</structfield> <type>name</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Name of the database containing this table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_read</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of database blocks read by vacuum operations performed on this table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_hit</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of times database blocks were found in the buffer cache by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_dirtied</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of database blocks dirtied by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_written</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of database blocks written by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>wal_records</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Total number of WAL records generated by vacuum operations performed on this table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>wal_fpi</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Total number of WAL full page images generated by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>wal_bytes</structfield> <type>numeric</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Total amount of WAL bytes generated by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>blk_read_time</structfield> <type>float8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Time spent reading blocks by vacuum operations, in milliseconds
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>blk_write_time</structfield> <type>float8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Time spent writing blocks by vacuum operations, in milliseconds
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>rel_blks_read</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of blocks vacuum operations read from this table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>rel_blks_hit</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of times blocks of this table were found in the buffer cache by vacuum
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>tuples_deleted</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of dead tuples vacuum operations deleted from this table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>pages_scanned</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of pages examined by vacuum operations. For append-optimized
       tables, the data scanned during compaction expressed in heap-equivalent
       pages of <xref linkend="guc-block-size"/> bytes.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>pages_removed</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of pages removed from physical storage by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>tuples_frozen</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of tuples that vacuum operations marked as frozen
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>recently_dead_tuples</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Cumulative number of dead tuples left by vacuum operations. For heap
       tables, these are tuples retained for transaction visibility. For
       append-optimized tables, these are hidden rows remaining in the AO
       visibility map after post-cleanup, including rows left because
       compaction is disabled or below its threshold. Each completed vacuum
       adds its remaining count, so the same row can be counted repeatedly;
       this is not a snapshot or a count of distinct rows.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>missed_dead_pages</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of pages that had at least one missed dead tuple
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>missed_dead_tuples</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of fully DEAD tuples that could not be pruned due to failure to acquire a cleanup lock
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>pages_frozen</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Cumulative number of heap pages on which vacuum froze at least one
       tuple. A page is counted again if a later vacuum freezes more tuples
       on it. Zero for append-optimized tables.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>pages_all_visible</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Cumulative number of heap pages whose visibility-map all-visible bit
       vacuum changed from unset to set, including empty pages. A page can
       be counted again after the bit is cleared by later changes. Rescanning
       an already all-visible page or setting only its all-frozen bit adds
       nothing. Zero for append-optimized tables.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>dead_pages</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Cumulative observations of heap pages containing dead tuples not yet
       removable, for example because of an old snapshot. Unlike
       <structfield>missed_dead_pages</structfield>, these tuples are not yet
       eligible for removal. Repeated observations add to this counter.
       Zero for append-optimized tables.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>freeze_age_vacuum_count</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of heap vacuums made aggressive by the XID or MultiXact freeze
       table age, including <command>VACUUM FREEZE</command>.
       <literal>DISABLE_PAGE_SKIPPING</literal> alone does not increment it.
       This is distinct from entering failsafe mode. Zero for append-optimized tables.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_file_segs</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of segment metadata entries observed at the end of the last
       append-optimized vacuum, including empty and awaiting-drop entries.
       This is a snapshot, not a cumulative counter. For column-oriented
       storage, each segment number is counted once, not once per column.
       Zero for heap tables and after a statistics reset.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>compacted_segments</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Cumulative number of append-optimized segments actually compacted.
       Segments considered but skipped are not counted. Zero for heap tables.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>tuples_moved</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Cumulative number of live tuples moved by append-optimized compaction.
       Zero for heap tables.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>awaiting_drop_segments</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of AO segment metadata entries still in <literal>AWAITING_DROP</literal>
       after the last completed vacuum. Replaced on each vacuum, not accumulated.
       For AOCS, counts segment numbers, not column files. Zero for heap and after reset.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-ao-tables">
  <title>The <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_ao_tables</structname> View</title>

  <indexterm zone="extvacuumstatistics">
   <secondary>pg_stats_vacuum_ao_tables</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   This view contains one row for each AO or AOCS parent table in the
   current database on the connected instance. Membership is determined by
   <structname>pg_appendonly</structname>, so heap tables, AO auxiliary heaps
   and indexes are excluded even after a statistics reset. Auxiliary tables
   remain in <structname>pg_stats_vacuum_tables</structname>, and indexes in
   <structname>pg_stats_vacuum_indexes</structname>.
  </para>

  <para>
   It includes the relation identifiers <structfield>relid</structfield>,
   <structfield>schema</structfield>, <structfield>relname</structfield> and
   <structfield>dbname</structfield>; total buffer, WAL and I/O timing fields;
   <structfield>rel_blks_read</structfield> and <structfield>rel_blks_hit</structfield>;
   and the applicable work counters <structfield>tuples_deleted</structfield>,
   <structfield>pages_scanned</structfield>, <structfield>pages_removed</structfield>,
   <structfield>recently_dead_tuples</structfield>, <structfield>total_file_segs</structfield>,
   <structfield>compacted_segments</structfield>, <structfield>tuples_moved</structfield>
   and <structfield>awaiting_drop_segments</structfield>. These fields have the
   same types and meaning as in <structname>pg_stats_vacuum_tables</structname>.
   Heap-only counters are omitted. The additional 27 columns below report
   resource usage separately for each AO vacuum phase.
  </para>

  <table id="extvacuumstatistics-ao-phase-columns">
   <title>AO Vacuum Phase Resource Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="1">
    <thead>
     <row><entry>Columns and meaning</entry></row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>ao_pre_cleanup_*</structfield>,
       <structfield>ao_compaction_*</structfield>,
       <structfield>ao_post_cleanup_*</structfield>
      </para>
      <para>
       Each phase prefix provides <literal>blks_read</literal>, <literal>blks_hit</literal>,
       <literal>blks_dirtied</literal>, <literal>blks_written</literal>,
       <literal>wal_records</literal> and <literal>wal_fpi</literal> as <type>int8</type>,
       <literal>wal_bytes</literal> as <type>numeric</type>, and
       <literal>blk_read_time</literal> and <literal>blk_write_time</literal> as
       <type>double precision</type> in milliseconds. These accumulate the
       phase's resource usage, excluding separately reported index work.
       Buffer counts include shared and local buffers, as in
       <structfield>total_blks_read</structfield>. I/O timing requires
       <varname>track_io_timing</varname>. Zero after reset.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>

  <para>
   The general and AO views read the same native work counters and extension
   resource entry. Extension resets clear resources and the awaiting-drop
   snapshot in both views while leaving native work intact. Resource totals
   and phase resources are read together from a single entry snapshot by
   <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_get_vacuum_ao_tables(dboid, reloid)</function>,
   including with <varname>stats_fetch_consistency</varname> set to
   <literal>none</literal>. Native work and resources are separate entries
   and need not reflect the same instant during a vacuum.
  </para>

  <para>
   On Cloudberry, use <structname>gp_stats_vacuum_ao_tables</structname> for rows
   from all instances and <structname>gp_stats_vacuum_ao_tables_summary</structname>
   for totals over primary segments, normalized for replicated tables. For
   example, run on the coordinator:
<programlisting>
SELECT relname, tuples_deleted, awaiting_drop_segments,
       ao_pre_cleanup_wal_bytes, ao_compaction_wal_bytes,
       ao_post_cleanup_wal_bytes
FROM ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_ao_tables_summary;
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-indexes">
  <title>The <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_indexes</structname> View</title>

  <indexterm zone="extvacuumstatistics">
   <secondary>pg_stats_vacuum_indexes</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   The view <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_indexes</structname>
   contains one row for each index in the current database, showing statistics
   about vacuuming that specific index.  The columns are shown in
   <xref linkend="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-indexes-columns"/>.
  </para>

  <table id="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-indexes-columns">
   <title><structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_indexes</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="1">
    <thead>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       Column Type
      </para>
      <para>
       Description
      </para></entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>indexrelid</structfield> <type>oid</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       OID of this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>schema</structfield> <type>name</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Name of the schema this index is in
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>indexrelname</structfield> <type>name</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Name of this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>dbname</structfield> <type>name</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Name of the database containing this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_read</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of database blocks read by vacuum operations while processing
       this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_hit</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of times database blocks were found in the buffer cache by
       vacuum operations while processing this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_dirtied</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of database blocks dirtied by vacuum operations while
       processing this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>total_blks_written</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of database blocks written by vacuum operations while
       processing this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>wal_records</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Total number of WAL records generated by vacuum operations while
       processing this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>wal_fpi</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Total number of WAL full page images generated by vacuum operations
       while processing this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>wal_bytes</structfield> <type>numeric</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Total amount of WAL bytes generated by vacuum operations while
       processing this index
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>blk_read_time</structfield> <type>float8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Time spent reading blocks by vacuum operations while processing this
       index, in milliseconds
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>blk_write_time</structfield> <type>float8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Time spent writing blocks by vacuum operations while processing this
       index, in milliseconds
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>rel_blks_read</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of blocks of this index read by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>rel_blks_hit</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of blocks of this index found in the buffer cache by vacuum
       operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>tuples_deleted</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of index entries removed by the bulkdelete passes of vacuums
       of the parent table
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>pages_deleted</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of index pages marked deleted by vacuum operations
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>dead_pages</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Cumulative observations of deleted index pages not yet reusable,
       sampled once after index cleanup as deleted pages minus reusable
       pages. Repeated observations add to this counter.
      </para></entry>
     </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-database">
  <title>The <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_database</structname> View</title>

  <indexterm zone="extvacuumstatistics">
   <secondary>pg_stats_vacuum_database</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   The view <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_database</structname>
   contains one row for each database in the cluster, showing aggregate vacuum
   statistics for that database.  Columns include
   <structfield>dboid</structfield>, <structfield>dbname</structfield>,
   <structfield>db_blks_read</structfield>, <structfield>db_blks_hit</structfield>,
   <structfield>db_blks_dirtied</structfield>, <structfield>db_blks_written</structfield>,
   WAL stats (<structfield>db_wal_records</structfield>,
   <structfield>db_wal_fpi</structfield>, <structfield>db_wal_bytes</structfield>),
   and I/O timing (<structfield>db_blk_read_time</structfield>,
   <structfield>db_blk_write_time</structfield>).
  </para>

  <table id="extvacuumstatistics-pg-stats-vacuum-database-columns">
   <title><structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.pg_stats_vacuum_database</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="1">
    <thead>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       Column Type
      </para>
      <para>
       Description
      </para></entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>dboid</structfield> <type>oid</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       OID of the database
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>dbname</structfield> <type>name</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Name of the database
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_blks_read</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of shared buffers read by vacuums in this database (tables and indexes)
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_blks_hit</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of shared buffer hits of vacuums in this database
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_blks_dirtied</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of shared buffers dirtied by vacuums in this database
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_blks_written</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of shared buffers written by vacuums in this database
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_wal_records</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of WAL records generated by vacuums in this database
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_wal_fpi</structfield> <type>int8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Number of WAL full page images generated by vacuums in this database
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_wal_bytes</structfield> <type>numeric</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Amount of WAL generated by vacuums in this database, in bytes
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_blk_read_time</structfield> <type>float8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Time spent reading data file blocks by vacuums in this database, in milliseconds (if <xref linkend="guc-track-io-timing"/> is enabled, otherwise zero)
      </para></entry>
     </row>
     <row>
      <entry role="catalog_table_entry"><para role="column_definition">
       <structfield>db_blk_write_time</structfield> <type>float8</type>
      </para>
      <para>
       Time spent writing data file blocks by vacuums in this database, in milliseconds (if <xref linkend="guc-track-io-timing"/> is enabled, otherwise zero)
      </para></entry>
     </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-functions">
  <title>Functions</title>

  <para>
   These functions reset only the extension's own metrics. Native VACUUM
   work counters and other core statistics are never reset by the module.
   Use <function>pg_stat_reset_vacuum_stats(relid)</function> for one relation
   or <function>pg_stat_reset_vacuum_stats()</function> for the current database
   and its non-shared relations. These preserve timing, invocation counts and
   other native statistics. On Cloudberry, use the corresponding
   <function>gp_stat_reset_vacuum_stats()</function> wrapper from the coordinator
   to include every primary segment. A native relation reset leaves extension resources intact;
   <function>pg_stat_reset()</function> clears both for the current database.
   The counters can cover different periods after a reset or a change in
   collection settings.
  </para>

  <para>
   Reset functions require superuser privileges by default. Access can be
   delegated with <command>GRANT EXECUTE</command>. Existing entries remain
   visible with zero extension counters after a reset; native work is unchanged; their table, index, or database
   type is preserved. Missing entries are not created by a reset.
   <function>pg_stat_reset()</function> also resets the module's counters for
   the current database.
  </para>

  <para>
   For Cloudberry, use the <literal>gp_</literal> wrappers from a normal
   coordinator connection to reset statistics across the cluster. These
   wrappers reset the coordinator and all primary segments. For example,
   to reset vacuum statistics for all databases across the cluster:
<programlisting>
SELECT ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_vacuum_statistics_reset();
</programlisting>
   Delegating access to a cluster wrapper also requires granting access to
   its corresponding local reset function.
  </para>

  <note>
   <para>
    Functions without the <literal>gp_</literal> prefix reset statistics
    <emphasis>only on the current node</emphasis>. Calling
    <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.vacuum_statistics_reset()</function>
    on the coordinator (QD) resets only the coordinator's counters;
    segment counters remain unchanged. The same applies to
    <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.extvac_reset_entry()</function> and
    <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.extvac_reset_db_entry()</function>.
    Use the local functions in a utility connection to an individual segment.
   </para>
  </note>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.vacuum_statistics_reset()</function>
     <returnvalue>void</returnvalue>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Resets all vacuum statistics of the module only on the current node,
      including relation and database statistics for all databases.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.extvac_reset_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter> <type>oid</type>, <parameter>relid</parameter> <type>oid</type>)</function>
     <returnvalue>void</returnvalue>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Resets the module's own vacuum metrics for one table or index only on the current
      node. Use
      <literal>0</literal> as <parameter>dboid</parameter> for a shared
      catalog. The database aggregate and other relations are unchanged.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.extvac_reset_db_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter> <type>oid</type>)</function>
     <returnvalue>void</returnvalue>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Resets the module's own vacuum metrics for a database only on the current node:
      its aggregate and the statistics of all its tables and indexes.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_vacuum_statistics_reset()</function>
     <returnvalue>void</returnvalue>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Calls <function>vacuum_statistics_reset()</function> on the coordinator
      and all primary segments, resetting statistics for all databases.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_extvac_reset_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter> <type>oid</type>, <parameter>relid</parameter> <type>oid</type>)</function>
     <returnvalue>void</returnvalue>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Calls <function>extvac_reset_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter>, <parameter>relid</parameter>)</function>
      on the coordinator and all primary segments, resetting statistics for
      one table or index. The database aggregate and other relations are
      unchanged.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term>
     <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_extvac_reset_db_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter> <type>oid</type>)</function>
     <returnvalue>void</returnvalue>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Calls <function>extvac_reset_db_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter>)</function>
      on the coordinator and all primary segments, resetting the database
      aggregate and the statistics of all its tables and indexes.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   To reset the current database across the cluster, run on the coordinator:
<programlisting>
SELECT ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_extvac_reset_db_entry(oid)
FROM pg_database WHERE datname = current_database();
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-append-optimized">
  <title>Append-Optimized Tables</title>

  <para>
   Append-optimized tables, row- and column-oriented, and their indexes are
   reported like heap tables, with the counters that have a meaning for
   their storage:
  </para>

  <para>
   Append-optimized vacuum time and cost delay sum active phases executed by
   the reporting worker, excluding gaps between phases. If the worker changes,
   only phases executed by the replacement worker are reported.
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <structfield>tuples_deleted</structfield> of the table is the number of
     dead tuples that the compaction of its segment files discarded, and
     <structfield>pages_removed</structfield> the space released by
     truncating segment files and dropping compacted ones, in heap-equivalent
     pages of <xref linkend="guc-block-size"/> bytes.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <structfield>pages_frozen</structfield>,
     <structfield>pages_all_visible</structfield>,
     <structfield>tuples_frozen</structfield>,
     <structfield>dead_pages</structfield>,
     <structfield>freeze_age_vacuum_count</structfield>,
     <structfield>missed_dead_tuples</structfield> and
     <structfield>missed_dead_pages</structfield> have no append-optimized
     counterpart and stay zero.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <structfield>recently_dead_tuples</structfield> accumulates the hidden
     rows left after each completed vacuum. Two vacuums that each leave
     100 hidden rows add 200; after compaction removes them, later vacuums
     add zero. The counter survives clean restarts and is cleared by
     statistics resets.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <structfield>compacted_segments</structfield> and
     <structfield>tuples_moved</structfield> accumulate the segments actually
     compacted and the live tuples moved. <structfield>pages_scanned</structfield>
     accumulates the scanned data in heap-equivalent pages, rounded up for
     each compacted segment. For row-oriented storage this uses the source
     segment EOF; for column-oriented storage it uses the scan's bytes read.
     <structfield>total_file_segs</structfield> instead records the segment
     metadata entry count at the end of the latest vacuum. It includes empty
     and awaiting-drop entries, and is not the number of physical column files.
     <structfield>awaiting_drop_segments</structfield> records only entries
     still in <literal>AWAITING_DROP</literal> at this point; older snapshots
     can prevent recycling them even after compaction completes.
     Native resets clear work counters and the total segment snapshot;
     extension resets clear resources and the awaiting-drop snapshot; the next
     vacuum refreshes the snapshot even if it performs no compaction.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The vacuum of an append-optimized table runs in phases (pre-cleanup,
     compaction, post-cleanup), each dispatched as a step of its own.  The
     buffer, WAL and I/O timing counters of the table cover all the phases
     of a vacuum, and the vacuum is reported once, at the end of the last
     one; a vacuum that fails in between reports nothing for the table.
     The <literal>ao_pre_cleanup_*</literal>, <literal>ao_compaction_*</literal>
     and <literal>ao_post_cleanup_*</literal> columns in the AO table views
     split these cumulative resources by phase. Their sum equals the corresponding table total,
     apart from floating-point rounding of I/O times. Phase counters exclude
     index resources and use the same replicated-table normalization as
     other table counters in cluster summaries. Independently rounded integer
     fields in replicated summaries can differ by rounding.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     The compaction moves the live tuples of a segment file to another one,
     so the index pass that follows removes the index entries of all tuples
     of the compacted file: <structfield>tuples_deleted</structfield> of an
     index counts the moved live tuples too, not only the dead ones.  As for
     heap tables, the resource usage of the index passes is reported with
     the indexes and not counted again with the table.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
 <para>
  AO auxiliary tables (<literal>aoseg</literal>, <literal>visimap</literal>,
  <literal>blkdir</literal>) have separate heap-statistics rows under their
  own OIDs; their indexes appear in the index views. Their later heap vacuum
  is not added to the parent AO phase counters. The auxiliary table OIDs in
  <structname>pg_appendonly</structname> associate these rows with the parent.
  Cluster summaries use that parent's replication divisor for auxiliary
  tables and their indexes.
 </para>

 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-cloudberry">
  <title>Using the Module in a Cloudberry Cluster</title>

  <para>
   Every instance of the cluster, the coordinator and each segment, keeps
   the statistics of the vacuums it runs itself: a <command>VACUUM</command>
   issued on the coordinator is dispatched to the segments, and each segment
   vacuums and counts its own part of the tables.  The
   <structname>pg_stats_vacuum_*</structname> views show the statistics of
   the instance they are queried on, so on the coordinator they show only
   what the coordinator itself vacuumed, which for distributed tables is next
   to nothing.  The module therefore also provides cluster-wide views, which
   query every instance:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_tables</structname>,
     <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_ao_tables</structname>,
     <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_indexes</structname> and
     <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_database</structname>
     have the columns of the corresponding <structname>pg_stats_vacuum_*</structname>
     view, preceded by <structfield>gp_segment_id</structfield>: one row per
     instance, <literal>-1</literal> being the coordinator, like the
     <structname>gp_stat_*</structname> views of the core.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_tables_summary</structname>,
     <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_ao_tables_summary</structname>
     and <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_indexes_summary</structname>
     have one row per relation, like the
     <structname>gp_stat_*_summary</structname> views of the core: the
     counters of user relations are summed over the segments, divided by the
     number of segments for replicated tables, which every segment stores and
     vacuums in full; system catalogs are shown as the coordinator counts
     them.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     <structname>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_stats_vacuum_database_summary</structname>
     sums the database aggregates over all instances, the coordinator
     included.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   Like the other <structname>gp_*</structname> views, the cluster-wide views
   must not be queried in utility mode, where they read the local instance
   twice.
  </para>

  <para>
   The reset functions without the <literal>gp_</literal> prefix act only on
   the connected instance.
   <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_vacuum_statistics_reset()</function>,
   <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_extvac_reset_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter>, <parameter>relid</parameter>)</function>
   and
   <function>ext_vacuum_statistics.gp_extvac_reset_db_entry(<parameter>dboid</parameter>)</function>
   call them on the coordinator and on every primary segment. Call these
   wrappers from a normal coordinator connection; use the local functions
   in utility mode. See <xref linkend="extvacuumstatistics-functions"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Install the matching extension library, control and SQL files in the
   Cloudberry installation on every host, including mirror and standby
   coordinator hosts. <command>gpconfig</command> updates configuration files;
   it does not distribute the extension files. The module must be preloaded
   on every instance so that it is available after promotion.
  </para>

  <para>
   Inspect the active setting and the configuration files:
<programlisting>
gpconfig -s shared_preload_libraries
gpconfig -s shared_preload_libraries --file
</programlisting>
   If the existing list is empty, set it as follows:
<programlisting>
gpconfig -c shared_preload_libraries -v 'ext_vacuum_statistics'
</programlisting>
   Otherwise pass the complete existing list with
   <literal>ext_vacuum_statistics</literal> appended. Preserve other preloaded
   libraries and any intentional differences between coordinator, primary
   and mirror settings. With <option>-v</option> and no role restriction,
   <command>gpconfig</command> updates the coordinator, standby, primaries and
   mirrors. Resolve any reported unreachable hosts before relying on their
   configuration. Check the resulting files and restart the cluster:
<programlisting>
gpconfig -s shared_preload_libraries --file
gpstop -ar
gpconfig -s shared_preload_libraries --file-compare
</programlisting>
   A reload with <command>gpstop -u</command> alone cannot load a new preload
   library. Check that the mirror and standby instances also restarted
   successfully. Then run
   <command>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS ext_vacuum_statistics</command>
   on the coordinator in each database where the views are needed; this
   command is dispatched to the segments.
  </para>

  <para>
   The warning above about starting without the module applies separately
   to each instance that has saved statistics of its custom kinds. Keep the
   module preloaded when disabling collection to preserve that instance's
   cumulative statistics.
  </para>

  <para>
   The counters are local <literal>pgstat</literal> state, not replicated
   through WAL. Preloading the module on a mirror enables collection after
   promotion but does not copy its primary's accumulated counters. A promoted
   instance reports its own local statistics and cannot continue the former
   primary's counter history. This also applies to standby coordinator
   promotion. A compatible clean restart can preserve an instance's own
   statistics; crash recovery resets them.
  </para>

  <para>
   Start a new measurement interval for an instance after promotion, even
   when its <structfield>gp_segment_id</structfield> is unchanged. Other
   instances keep their counters, but cluster summaries can decrease when
   a primary is replaced. Monitoring should retain earlier samples externally
   and establish a new baseline after failover; the discontinuity does not
   represent negative vacuum work.
  </para>

  <para>
   <varname>vacuum_statistics.enabled</varname> is read by the instance that
   runs the vacuum.  A <command>SET</command> on the coordinator is passed on
   to the segments of the session; to stop collecting extension resources in the whole cluster,
   set it with <command>gpconfig</command> and reload the configuration
   (<command>gpstop -u</command>).
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="extvacuumstatistics-configuration">
  <title>Configuration Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><varname>vacuum_statistics.enabled</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Enables collection of extension resource metrics. Native work counters
      obey <varname>track_counts</varname> independently.  Default: <literal>on</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect2>
</sect1>
